('Herbs of Gold', 'Biotin 3mg', 'supplement', ARRAY['Biotin (vitamin B7) 3mg']::text[], ARRAY['Biotin, also known as vitamin B7, forms part of the B vitamin family. Biotin is involved in cell growth, fatty acid production and in the metabolism of carbohydrates, fats and proteins.','Biotin is a water-soluble vitamin and is an essential cofactor for 5 carboxylase enzymes including:','Acetyl-coenzyme A (CoA) carboxylase 1 and acetyl-CoA carboxylase 2: binds bicarbonate to acetyl-CoA to form malonyl-CoA, required for the production of fatty acids.','Pyruvate carboxylase: critical for gluconeogenesis (formation of glucose from sources other than carbohydrates such as amino acids and fats).','Methylcrotonyl-CoQ carboxylase: essential step in the metabolism of the amino acid leucine.','Propionyl-CoA carboxylase: catalyses essential steps in the metabolism of amino acids, cholesterol and odd chain fatty acids.','Many foods contain small amounts of biotin and this depends greatly on the season. Foods that contain the most biotin include organ meats, eggs, fish, meat, seeds, nuts and certain vegetables including sweet potato.','Gastrointestinal enzymes break down the protein-bound forms of ingested biotin, which then undergo further processing by the','enzyme biotinidase in the intestinal lumen to release free biotin. The free biotin is then absorbed in the small intestine and most biotin is stored in the liver and central nervous system.','A diet high in raw egg whites can lead to a biotin deficiency. Raw egg whites are high in avidin, a protein that binds to biotin and inhibits its absorption. Cooking denatures avidin, making it unable to interfere with biotin absorption.','Signs and symptoms of biotin deficiency include skin rashes, brittle nails, thinning hair or hair loss.','Pregnancy increases biotin requirements as the fetus requires biotin for carboxylase reactions and DNA replication.','Hair, skin & nails','Biotin 3mg supports hair, skin and nail health. It plays a role in the normal structure and function of skin and mucous membranes. Biotin is a coenzyme for carboxylase enzymes that are involved in fatty acid synthesis, branched-chain amino acid catabolism and','gluconeogenesis, important for maintenance of healthy skin and hair.','Keratin is an important structural protein found in the epidermis. It is the key component of the hair, skin and nails. Keratin mainly functions to adhere cells to each other and to form a protective layer on the outside of the skin. Biotin plays a role in protein synthesis, specifically keratin production.','Biotin 3mg supports nail health, strength and thickness. Brittle nails are characterised by roughness of the surface of the nail plate, fragility and peeling. It is associated with an abnormality of keratin,','keratin associated proteins, water, and/or lipid content. Biotin appears to improve brittle nails by inducing epidermal differentiation.','In one study, 71 subjects with brittle fingernails were given 2.5mg of biotin per day. Out of the 45 subjects that completed the study, 91% showed a definite improvement with firmer and harder fingernails after an average treatment of 5.5±2.3 months1.','Another study investigated the effects of 2.5mg/day of biotin on brittle and splitting fingernails. A total of 32 subjects were placed into 3 groups: group A was the control (n=10), group B comprised subjects with brittle nails (n=8) studied before and after biotin supplementation, and group C included subjects with brittle nails in whom the administration of biotin did not coincide exactly with the initial and terminal clipping of the nails (n=14). The thickness of the nails in group B increased significantly by 25% and group C by 7%. Splitting of the nails were reduced in groups B and C and the irregular cellular arrangement of the dorsal surface of brittle nails became more regular in all nails of group B and in 8 of 11 nails of group C. Results were seen after 6 to 15 months of biotin supplementation2.','hair nail support']::text[], ARRAY['Biotin shares the sodium-dependent multivitamin transporter (SMVT) with vitamin B5. Theoretically, this may reduce vitamin B5 absorption due to competitive inhibition of the transporter.']::text[], ARRAY['60 tablets']::text[], NULL, true, NULL, 'HOG-0012', ARRAY['hair_nail_support']::text[], ARRAY[]::text[], ARRAY[]::text[], ARRAY[]::text[]),
('Herbs of Gold', 'Breastfeeding Support', 'supplement', ARRAY['Trigonella foenum-graecum (Fenugreek) seed extract 750mg','Cnicus benedictus (Blessed thistle) herb extract 250mg']::text[], ARRAY['Breastfeeding Support contains two herbs, Fenugreek and Blessed thistle, to support milk supply in breastfeeding women.','Fenugreek and Blessed thistle are herbal galactagogues. A galactagogue is a substance that promotes the secretion and flow of milk.','A 2015 qualitative study found that Fenugreek and Blessed thistle were the two most commonly used herbal galactagogues1.','Breastfeeding','During pregnancy, breasts change and develop to be ready to supply milk. The amount of breastmilk usually increases a few days after the birth, when the milk “comes in”.','The first milk produced after the birth is called colostrum, which is more concentrated than mature milk and rich in protein and antibodies. It is gradually replaced by mature milk.','When a baby sucks at the breast, it stimulates nerves in the nipple that release hormones into the blood stream. Prolactin activates milk-making tissues, while oxytocin causes the breast to let down the milk (“let-down reflex”).','Breastmilk is the normal food for babies, containing all the baby’s nutritional needs for the first 6 months of life, satisfying both hunger and thirst, and supporting the baby’s immune system. The act of breastfeeding also helps the baby’s jaw development. Breastfeeding benefits the mother by helping the uterus return to its normal size after childbirth.','One of the most common reasons mothers cease breastfeeding earlier than planned is because they feel they don’t have enough milk for their babies.','Fenugreek','Fenugreek is a herb native to the Mediterranean region, southern Europe and western Asia. It is traditionally used in Western herbal medicine as a nutritive tonic and galactagogue to increase breastmilk production in breastfeeding women.','The mechanism of action of Fenugreek on breastmilk is not fully understood, but it is suggested that Fenugreek stimulates sweat production, which then enhances milk secretion because the breast is a type of sweat gland. Fenugreek may also have oestrogenic activity.','A randomised, double-blind, placebo-controlled study involving 66 mother-infant pairs assessed the effect of Fenugreek tea on breastmilk production. The mean measured breastmilk volume of','the mothers who received galactagogue tea was significantly higher than the placebo and control groups (p<0.05)2.','An anecdotal report of 1200 women taking 580mg or 610mg of Fenugreek three times a day stated that most women experienced an increase in milk supply within 24 to 72 hours of use3.','Fenugreek also supports healthy appetite.','Blessed thistle','Blessed thistle supports breastmilk production in breastfeeding women. It is believed to work by stimulating the flow of blood to the mammary glands, thereby enriching milk flow.','Blessed thistle is a weed native to the Mediterranean that has been used for over 2000 years to stimulate appetite, aid digestion and as a galactagogue. According to Grieves, “it is chiefly used now for nursing mothers the warm infusion scarcely ever failing to procure a proper supply of milk. It is considered one of the best medicines which can','be used for the purpose”4.','blood sugar support, breastfeeding review required']::text[], ARRAY['Contraindicated in pregnancy - Fenugreek can cause uterine contractions, increasing the risk of early labour or miscarriage.']::text[], ARRAY['60 tablets']::text[], NULL, true, NULL, 'HOG-0013', ARRAY['blood_sugar_support (review)','breastfeeding_review_required']::text[], ARRAY['antidiabetic','pregnancy_review_required']::text[], ARRAY['antidiabetic']::text[], ARRAY[]::text[]),
